{"id": "trm:snataka", "term": "snātaka", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The last of the five kinds of nirgrantha named in TS 9.46.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.46"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:chedopasthapana", "term": "chedopasthāpanā", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "cutting and re-establishing", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The second of the five kinds of cāritra in the Digambara list (TS 9.18).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.18"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:pariharavisuddhi", "term": "parihāra-viśuddhi", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "purity of avoidance", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The third of the five kinds of cāritra (TS 9.18).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.18"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:suksmasamparaya-caritra", "term": "sūkṣma-sāmparāya (as a kind of cāritra)", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "subtle passion", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The fourth of the five kinds of cāritra (TS 9.18); the same name is used of a stage at which the passions are subtle (9.10).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.18", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.10"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:smrti-samanvahara", "term": "smṛti-samanvāhāra", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "continuous bringing to mind", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.30 the continuous bringing to mind that makes up ārta dhyāna, directed at separation from a disagreeable thing.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.30"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:pratisevana", "term": "pratisevanā", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "resorting to; transgression", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One of the eight heads (TS 9.47) by which the kinds of nirgrantha are to be distinguished; the sūtra gives no definition (the commentators read it as transgression of the vows).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.47"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:tirtha-jain", "term": "tīrtha (Jain)", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "ford", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.47 and 10.9, a head under which ascetics and the liberated are set out; the commentators read it as the Jina's dispensation. Not the pilgrimage-place sense of tīrtha.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.47", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:10.9"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:antaraya-karma", "term": "antarāya (obstructive karma)", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "obstruction", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The obstructive karma, one of the four whose destruction gives omniscience (TS 10.1); not the antarāya of the Yoga Sūtra.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:10.1"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:samvara", "term": "saṃvara", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "stopping", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.1 the stopping (nirodha) of inflow; effected by the guptis, samitis, dharma, anuprekṣās, conquest of hardships and cāritra (9.2), and by tapas (9.3, ca).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.1", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.2", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.3"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:gupti", "term": "gupti", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "control", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.4 the proper restraint of activity (yoga) of body, speech and mind; first of the means of saṃvara (9.2).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.4", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.2"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:samiti", "term": "samiti", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "carefulness", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.5 five heads: īryā, bhāṣā, eṣaṇā, ādāna-nikṣepa, utsarga (walking, speaking, seeking, taking up and setting down, letting go).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.5"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:anupreksa", "term": "anuprekṣā", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "repeated contemplation", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In TS 9.7 the repeated thinking on twelve themes: impermanence, no refuge, saṃsāra, aloneness, otherness, impurity, inflow, stopping, shedding, the universe, rarity of enlightenment, and the well-taught dharma. Not the Buddhist or Yoga homonym.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.7"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:dhyana", "term": "dhyāna", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "meditation", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "In Jainism (TS 9.27) the restraining of thought on one point, for one of the best constitution, up to an antarmuhūrta; fourfold: ārta, raudra, dharmya, śukla (9.28), the last two causes of liberation (9.29).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.27", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.28", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.29"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:arta-dhyana", "term": "ārta-dhyāna", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "pained meditation", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "TS 9.30-9.34: continuous bringing to mind on separation from the disagreeable, the reverse for the agreeable, on pain, and nidāna; it belongs to the unrestrained, the partly restrained and the careless ascetic.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.30", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.31", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.32", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.33", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.34"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:raudra-dhyana", "term": "raudra-dhyāna", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "fierce meditation", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "TS 9.35: dhyāna arising from violence, falsehood, theft and guarding of sense-objects; it belongs to the unrestrained and the partly restrained.", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.35"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:dharma-dhyana", "term": "dharma-dhyāna", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "meditation of dharma", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "TS 9.36: dharmya dhyāna is for the examination of the command, the harm, the ripening and the form; with śukla, a cause of liberation (9.29).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.36", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.29"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:sukla-dhyana", "term": "śukla-dhyāna", "language": "Sanskrit", "literal": "pure meditation", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "TS 9.37-9.44: four kinds (9.39), the first two for one who knows the pūrvas and the last two for the kevalin (9.37-9.38), the first two with vitarka (scriptural knowledge) and the second without vīcāra (9.41-9.44).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.37", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.38", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.39", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.41", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.42", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.43", "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.44"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
